# Project: pytest Python Test Automation Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use pytest as the primary test runner for Python projects
- Organize tests in a dedicated `tests/` directory mirroring the source layout
- Use `conftest.py` files for shared fixtures and hooks at each directory level
- Separate unit, integration, and end-to-end tests into distinct sub-directories

## Coding Standards
- Follow PEP 8 and use type hints throughout test code
- Use descriptive test function names that express intent: `test_user_login_with_valid_credentials`
- Keep test functions focused on a single behavior
- Limit nesting inside test functions; prefer fixture composition over setup blocks

## Test Organization
- Prefix all test files with `test_` and all test functions/classes with `test_`
- Group related tests inside `TestClass` only when shared state or fixtures justify it
- Use `pytest.mark` for categorizing tests (e.g., `@pytest.mark.unit`, `@pytest.mark.integration`, `@pytest.mark.slow`)
- Register custom marks in `pytest.ini` or `pyproject.toml` to avoid warnings

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern in every test
- Keep each test independent; never rely on execution order
- Prefer explicit assertions with descriptive failure messages
- Avoid logic (loops, conditionals) inside tests; move complexity to helpers or fixtures

## Fixtures
- Define fixtures in `conftest.py` for cross-module sharing
- Use the appropriate fixture scope: `function` (default), `class`, `module`, `session`
- Implement teardown via `yield` in fixtures, not `return`
- Prefer dependency injection through fixture parameters over global state

## Mocking and Patching
- Use `pytest-mock` (the `mocker` fixture) for mocking instead of `unittest.mock` directly
- Patch at the point of use, not at the definition
- Use `mocker.patch.object` for patching specific attributes on real objects
- Use `responses` or `httpretty` libraries for mocking HTTP requests in integration tests

## Parametrization
- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` to cover multiple input/output cases without duplicating tests
- Provide `ids` for parametrize to make test output readable
- Combine parametrization with fixtures for data-driven testing scenarios
- Extract large parameter sets into module-level constants or external files

## Assertions
- Use plain `assert` statements; rely on pytest's assertion introspection for clear failure output
- Import `pytest.approx` for floating-point comparisons
- Use `pytest.raises` as a context manager to assert expected exceptions
- Use `pytest.warns` to assert expected warnings

## Coverage
- Integrate `pytest-cov` for coverage measurement (`pytest --cov=src --cov-report=html`)
- Set minimum coverage thresholds in `pyproject.toml` (`[tool.coverage.report] fail_under = 80`)
- Exclude generated files, migrations, and non-testable boilerplate from coverage
- Track branch coverage in addition to line coverage

## Performance
- Mark slow tests with `@pytest.mark.slow` and exclude them from default runs
- Use `pytest-xdist` for parallel test execution (`-n auto`)
- Minimise I/O and network calls in unit tests; stub or mock external dependencies
- Use `session`-scoped fixtures for expensive setup shared across all tests

## Test Data Management
- Use `factory_boy` or `pytest-factoryboy` for generating test model instances
- Store static fixtures in `tests/fixtures/` as JSON/YAML files and load them via fixtures
- Use `Faker` for generating realistic but random test data
- Never commit real credentials or PII in test data files

## Configuration
- Configure pytest in `pyproject.toml` under `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`
- Set `testpaths`, `python_files`, `python_classes`, and `python_functions` explicitly
- Use `addopts` for default flags (e.g., `-v --strict-markers`)
- Manage environment variables with `pytest-dotenv` or `monkeypatch.setenv`

## CI/CD Integration
- Output JUnit XML reports for CI pipelines (`--junitxml=reports/junit.xml`)
- Run the full suite on pull requests; run only fast tests on every commit using markers
- Cache the `.pytest_cache` directory across CI runs for faster reruns
- Fail the pipeline if coverage drops below the defined threshold

## Error Handling
- Test both the happy path and error/edge-case paths for every public function
- Assert on specific exception types and messages, not just that an exception was raised
- Use `pytest.raises(ExceptionType, match=r"pattern")` for precise exception matching
- Test boundary conditions explicitly (empty inputs, None, max values)

## Documentation
- Write a short docstring on complex test functions explaining *why*, not *what*
- Maintain a `tests/README.md` describing how to run tests, required environment variables, and marker conventions
- Document custom fixtures and their expected return types in `conftest.py`
- Include example commands for common test scenarios in the project README

Remember to keep the test suite fast, deterministic, and easy to run locally with a single `pytest` command.
